description:
The Bachelor of Arts in Narrative Studies program, offered by the Department of Comparative Literature, trains students in the humanistic study of narrative in its diverse forms and media. Narrative is considered as a fundamental human activity of making meaning by telling stories, constructing events, designing plots, and devising characters and agents. The program emphasizes an interdisciplinary approach to the study of narrative, and its curriculum focuses on the theory, history, and practice of narrative, broadly conceived, across a range of genres, disciplines, and media.

Students will:

*   gain an understanding of narrative as a fundamental human activity for making meaning;
*   acquire an understanding of the historical evolution of narrative forms and genres;
*   develop an analytical vocabulary for discussing diverse narratives;
*   learn to relate the study of narrative to their own specific interests and to their chosen professional paths; and
*   develop their skills in crafting narratives of their own in a variety of modes.
